ach proposed token and for the next token after the last proposal.\n\nThe method can preserve the target model\u2019s output distribution by treating the draft only as a proposal, not as the final answer. For each proposed token, the target model compares its own probability for that token with the draft probability. If the target is sufficiently likely, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a replacement token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. This rejection-sampling step ensures that the final sequence is distributed exactly as if the target model had generated every token one at a time, assuming the same random seed and sampling parameters.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the cost of running the draft model, the cost of the target model\u2019s verification pass, and hardware efficiency. A good draft model should be cheap to run and closely aligned with the target model\u2019s preferences. The verification pass must also be efficient, especially when batched or on memory-bound hardware.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low acceptance rates, caused by a draft model that is too small, poorly aligned, or used on difficult prompts, and high overhead from running the draft model or verification pass, especially when only one or two tokens are accepted per iteration.\n\nCount words:\nSpeculative1 decoding2 speeds3 up4 large5 language6 model7 inference", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0017918895464390516], [22916, -6.787217140197754]], [[1144, -8.463895028398838e-06], [513, -12.654343605041504]], [[4087, -0.2329234480857849], [310, -1.5826282501220703]], [[1156, -0.0017926060827448964], [303, -6.432986736297607]], [[579, -0.22100742161273956], [25, -1.821619987487793]], [[1622, -0.010597875341773033], [3296, -4.761072635650635]], [[25, -0.2959153950214386], [13, -1.38